# Nornir deployment performance envelope

Factor this hardware and I/O profile into performance, caching, and parallelism choices.

## Dataset and storage

- Production volumes are very large (**~100 TB** scale), typically on **NAS**.
- Network path is often about **10 Gbps**; treat disk and network I/O as **high-latency and costly**.
- Prefer sequential/streaming access, fewer round-trips, and bounded concurrency over many tiny random reads/writes on NAS.
- Do not assume local-disk latency for paths on the volume root.

## Local SSD cache (`/tmp`)

- When hot data must leave NAS, use **local SSD caches under `/tmp`** (or the configured Nornir temp root).
- **Document size requirements** for any such cache (expected working set and worst case).
- Budget for caches in the **512 GB–1 TB** range when the algorithm needs large local scratch; do not silently assume unlimited `/tmp`.

## Memory and paging

- Plan on roughly **2 GB RAM per CPU core** (sometimes **~3 GB/core**).
- Design peak resident set and pool sizing against that envelope; spill or chunk before relying on anonymous RAM growth.
- Paging, when it occurs, is on **SSD or better**—still treat heavy swap as a failure mode for hot loops, not a normal strategy.

## GPU

- Typical GPUs are **Ada RTX 4500 class or better**.
- GPU paths should assume that class of VRAM/throughput; still minimize host↔device transfers (see NumPy/CuPy rule).

## Decision checklist

- Will this add many small NAS IOPS? Batch, coalesce, or stage to `/tmp` first.
- If using `/tmp`, is capacity (up to **512 GB–1 TB**) documented for operators?
- Does parallelism × working set fit **~2–3 GB/core** without pathological swapping?
- Does a GPU path pay for itself after transfer cost on Ada-4500-class hardware?

### Related rules

- Streaming and memory-bounded processing (chunking, backpressure, spill-to-disk).
- NumPy/CuPy compatibility (execution locality, transfer cost).
